 their dependency metrics against the single observed backend latency point is the most decisive untried causal source, and it does not re-query the exhausted database scope.\", \"expected_discriminator\": \"Redis or payment error/latency rising before or alongside the backend deadline breach supports a dependency-origin mechanism (redis_unavailable or payment_external_timeout); redis and payment metrics remaining flat or absent while backend degrades supports a backend-origin mechanism (backend_resource_saturation or backend_pool_config_regression); if both dependency metrics are unavailable, that leaves the backend-origin versus postgres-origin split to the concurrent database probe.\"}], \"hypothesis_updates\": [], \"selected_hypothesis_id\": null, \"stop_reason\": null}, {\"action\": \"INVESTIGATE\", \"reason\": \"Round 3 completed task-0005 (postgres get_db_lock_waits and get_db_health_metrics, both completed ok with record_count 0) and task-0006 (redis and payment query_metrics, both completed ok with record_count 0); together with the earlier database and unfiltered change scopes these are completed but empty scopes, i.e. evidence gaps rather than proof of health, and the backend and gateway log scopes plus the backend metric scope are already completed and unfiltered, so re-querying them cannot surface new records. No hypothesis has a cause-specific discriminator and all three (backend_resource_saturation, redis_unavailable, payment_external_timeout) remain OPEN, so neither FINALIZE nor a supported discrimination is yet available. The redis, payment, and postgres service log streams have never been queried; these are genuinely new causal sources (distinct services, not narrower filters inside an exhausted scope) whose error signatures and chronology can localize the failing edge. Budget is ample (37 remaining requests, 5 rounds, 21 tool calls, 18 affordable tasks), so assign two discriminating log tasks; the requested_checks for severity-free backend/gateway log resweeps and redis/payment metric re-queries are declined as redundant with completed scopes. Reserve the final decision and review calls.\", \"assignments\": [{\"task_id\": \"task-0007\", \"role\": \"logs\", \"question\": \"During the incident observation window 2026-10-01T12:00:00Z-13:00:00Z, do redis and payment service log streams show error, timeout, or connection-failure signatures, and at what timestamps relative to the backend deadline breach at 12:00:14Z, so as to separate redis_unavailable or payment_external_timeout from a backend-origin mechanism?\", \"allowed_tools\": [\"query_logs\"], \"filters\": {\"service\": \"redis\", \"start_ts\": \"2026-10-01T12:00:00Z\", \"end_ts\": \"2026-10-01T13:00:00Z\", \"limit\": 50}, \"reason\": \"The redis and payment query_metrics scopes (task-0006) returned completed but empty results, so those metric sources are exhausted while their service log streams have never been queried. Log error text and chronology in the two declared backend dependencies (topology incident_1003-T-backend) is the most decisive untried way to test whether the failing edge is dependency-origin or backend-local, and it does not re-read any completed scope.\", \"expected_discriminator\": \"Redis timeout or connection-refused signatures preceding or coinciding with 12:00:14Z support redis_unavailable; payment timeout/error signatures in the same interval support payment_external_timeout; a dependency log stream with no error while backend reports a deadline breach keeps backend_resource_saturation as the leading explanation and preserves the dependency hypotheses as evidence gaps; the ordering of the first dependency error relative to the first backend error establishes which side failed first.\"}, {\"task_id\": \"task-0008\", \"role\": \"logs\", \"question\": \"During the incident observation window 2026-10-01T12:00:00Z-13:00:00Z, does the postgres service log stream show connection exhaustion, lock contention, slow-query, or saturation signatures that would make the backend deadline breach at 12:00:14Z a downstream symptom of a database-origin failure rather than a backend-local or dependency-origin cause?\", \"allowed_tools\": [\"query_logs\"], \"filters\": {\"service\": \"postgres\", \"start_ts\": \"2026-10-01T12:00:00Z\", \"end_ts\": \"2026-10-01T13:00:00Z\", \"limit\": 50}, \"reason\": \"The postgres service log stream has never been queried, while database_health_normal is false and the postgres connection, active-session, lock-wait, and health-metric scopes all returned completed empty results. Per the contract an empty broad query is an evidence gap, not proof of health, so the one untried postgres source is the correct pivot before concluding the database is uninvolved, and it avoids re-reading the exhausted database metric scopes.\", \"expected_discriminator\": \"Connection-exhaustion or saturation signatures support a postgres-origin mechanism (postgres_connection_exhaustion); lock-wait or prolonged slow-query signatures support postgres_lock_contention or postgres_slow_query with the database as the upstream origin of the backend symptom; an empty postgres log stream alongside the empty database and dependency-metric scopes keeps the database domain an open evidence gap and shifts the decisive test back to the backend-local versus redis/payment dependency split.\"}], \"hypothesis_updates\": [], \"selected_hypothesis_id\": null, \"stop_reason\": null}], \"events\": [], \"report\": null, \"stop_reason\": null, \"open_questions\": [], \"requested_checks\": [{\"task_id\": \"task-0004\", \"role\": \"logs\", \"question\": \"Re-query backend logs across the full window without a severity filter to recover any pool wait/timeout, thread-exhaustion, or dependency connection-failure lines that ERROR-only filtering may hide.\", \"allowed_tools\": [\"query_logs\"], \"filters\": {\"service\": \"backend\", \"start_ts\": \"2026-10-01T12:00:00Z\", \"end_ts\": \"2026-10-01T13:00:00Z\", \"limit\": 50}, \"reas